sn47.17:1.8 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn47.19
sn47.19:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sumbhas, near the town of the Sumbhas named Sedaka. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling among the Sumbhas, where there was a town of the Sumbhas named Sedaka.
sn47.19:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn47.19:1.3 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, a pole acrobat of the corpse-workers set up the bamboo pole of his tribe and said to his apprentice Medakathālikā, | T: “Bhikkhus, once in the past an acrobat set up his bamboo pole and addressed his apprentice Medakathalika thus:
sn47.19:1.4 | S: ‘Come now, dear Medakathālikā, climb up the bamboo pole and stand above my shoulders.’ | T: ‘Come, dear Medakathalika, climb the bamboo pole and stand on my shoulders.’
sn47.19:1.5 | S: ‘Yes, tutor,’ she replied. She climbed up the bamboo pole and stood above her tutor’s shoulders. | T: Having replied, ‘Yes, teacher,’ the apprentice Medakathalika climbed up the bamboo pole and stood on the teacher’s shoulders.
sn47.19:1.6 | S: Then the pole acrobat of the corpse-workers said to Medakathālikā, | T: The acrobat then said to the apprentice Medakathalika:
sn47.19:1.7 | S: ‘You look after me, dear Medakathālikā, and I shall look after you. | T: ‘You protect me, dear Medakathalika, and I’ll protect you.
sn47.19:1.8 | S: That’s how, guarding and looking after each other, we’ll display our skill, collect our fee, and get down safely from the bamboo pole.’ | T: Thus guarded by one another, protected by one another, we’ll display our skills, collect our fee, and get down safely from the bamboo pole.’
sn47.19:1.9 | S: When he said this, Medakathālikā said to her teacher, | T: When this was said, the apprentice Medakathalika replied:
sn47.19:1.10 | S: ‘That’s not how it will be, tutor! | T: ‘That’s not the way to do it, teacher.
sn47.19:1.11 | S: You look after yourself, and I shall look after myself. | T: You protect yourself, teacher, and I’ll protect myself.
sn47.19:1.12 | S: That’s how, guarding and looking after ourselves, we’ll display our skill, collect our fee, and get down safely from the bamboo pole.’ | T: Thus, each self-guarded and self-protected, we’ll display our skills, collect our fee, and get down safely from the bamboo pole.’
sn47.19:2.1 | S: That’s the way,” said the Buddha. “It’s just as Medakathālikā said to her tutor. | T: “That’s the method there,” the Blessed One said. “It’s just as the apprentice Medakathalika said to the teacher.
sn47.19:2.2 | S: Thinking ‘I’ll look after myself,’ you should cultivate mindfulness meditation. | T: ‘I will protect myself,’ bhikkhus: thus should the establishments of mindfulness be practised.
sn47.19:2.3 | S: Thinking ‘I’ll look after others,’ you should cultivate mindfulness meditation. | T: ‘I will protect others,’ bhikkhus: thus should the establishments of mindfulness be practised.
sn47.19:2.4 | S: Looking after yourself, you look after others; and looking after others, you look after yourself. | T: Protecting oneself, bhikkhus, one protects others; protecting others, one protects oneself.
sn47.19:3.1 | S: And how do you look after others by looking after yourself? | T: “And how is it, bhikkhus, that by protecting oneself one protects others?
sn47.19:3.2 | S: By development, cultivation, and practice of meditation. | T: By the pursuit, development, and cultivation of the four establishments of mindfulness.
sn47.19:3.3 | P: evaṁ kho, bhikkhave, attānaṁ rakkhanto paraṁ rakkhati. | T: It is in such a way that by protecting oneself one protects others.
sn47.19:3.4 | S: And how do you look after yourself by looking after others? | T: “And how is it, bhikkhus, that by protecting others one protects oneself?
sn47.19:3.5 | S: By acceptance, harmlessness, love, and sympathy. | T: By patience, harmlessness, lovingkindness, and sympathy.
sn47.19:3.6 | P: evaṁ kho, bhikkhave, paraṁ rakkhanto attānaṁ rakkhati. | T: It is in such a way that by protecting others one protects oneself.
sn47.19:3.7 | S: Thinking ‘I’ll look after myself,’ you should cultivate mindfulness meditation. | T: “‘I will protect myself,’ bhikkhus: thus should the establishments of mindfulness be practised.
sn47.19:3.8 | S: Thinking ‘I’ll look after others,’ you should cultivate mindfulness meditation. | T: ‘I will protect others,’ bhikkhus: thus should the establishments of mindfulness be practised.
sn47.19:3.9 | S: Looking after yourself, you look after others; and looking after others, you look after yourself.” | T: Protecting oneself, bhikkhus, one protects others; protecting others, one protects oneself.”
sn47.19:3.10 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn47.1
sn47.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn47.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Vesālī in Ambapālī’s Mango Grove. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Vesali in Ambapali’s Grove.
sn47.1:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn47.1:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Bhikkhus!”
sn47.1:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Venerable sir!” the bhikkhus replied.
sn47.1:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn47.1:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, the four kinds of mindfulness meditation are the path to convergence. They are in order to purify sentient beings, to get past sorrow and crying, to make an end of pain and sadness, to discover the system, and to realize extinguishment. | T: “Bhikkhus, this is the one-way path for the purification of beings, for the overcoming of sorrow and lamentation, for the passing away of pain and displeasure, for the achievement of the method, for the realization of Nibbāna, that is, the four establishments of mindfulness.
sn47.1:2.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn47.1:2.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: “Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.1:2.4 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: He dwells contemplating feelings in feelings, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.1:2.5 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of the mind—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: He dwells contemplating mind in mind, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.1:2.6 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: He dwells contemplating phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.1:2.7 | S: The four kinds of mindfulness meditation are the path to convergence. They are in order to purify sentient beings, to get past sorrow and crying, to make an end of pain and sadness, to discover the system, and to realize extinguishment.” | T: “This, bhikkhus, is the one-way path for the purification of beings, for the overcoming of sorrow and lamentation, for the passing away of pain and displeasure, for the achievement of the method, for the realization of Nibbāna, that is, the four establishments of mindfulness.”
sn47.1:3.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn47.1:3.2 | S: Satisfied, the mendicants approved what the Buddha said. | T: Elated, those bhikkhus delighted in the Blessed One’s statement.
sn47.1:3.3 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn47.20
sn47.20:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn47.20:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sumbhas, near the town of the Sumbhas named Sedaka. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was living among the Sumbhas, where there was a town of the Sumbhas named Sedaka.
sn47.20:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn47.20:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Bhikkhus!”
sn47.20:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Venerable sir!” the bhikkhus replied.
sn47.20:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn47.20:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, suppose that on hearing, ‘The finest lady in the land! The finest lady in the land!’ a large crowd would gather. | T: “Bhikkhus, suppose that on hearing, ‘The most beautiful girl of the land! The most beautiful girl of the land!’ a great crowd of people would assemble.
sn47.20:2.2 | S: And the finest lady in the land would dance and sing in a most thrilling way. | T: Now that most beautiful girl of the land would dance exquisitely and sing exquisitely.
sn47.20:2.3 | S: On hearing, ‘The finest lady in the land is dancing and singing! The finest lady in the land is dancing and singing!’ an even larger crowd would gather. | T: On hearing, ‘The most beautiful girl of the land is dancing! The most beautiful girl of the land is singing!’ an even larger crowd of people would assemble.
sn47.20:2.4 | S: Then a person would come along who wants to live and doesn’t want to die, who wants to be happy and recoils from pain. | T: Then a man would come along, wishing to live, not wishing to die, wishing for happiness, averse to suffering.
sn47.20:2.5 | S: They’d say to him, | T: Someone would say to him:
sn47.20:2.6 | S: ‘My man, this is a bowl full to the brim with oil. You must carry it in between this large crowd and the finest lady in the land. | T: ‘Good man, you must carry around this bowl of oil filled to the brim between the crowd and the most beautiful girl of the land.
sn47.20:2.7 | S: And a man with a drawn sword will follow right behind you. | T: A man with a drawn sword will be following right behind you,
sn47.20:2.8 | S: Wherever you spill even a drop, he’ll chop off your head right there.’ | T: and wherever you spill even a little of it, right there he will fell your head.’
sn47.20:2.9 | S: What do you think, mendicants? | T: “What do you think, bhikkhus,
sn47.20:2.10 | S: Would that person lose focus on that bowl, and negligently get distracted outside?” | T: would that man stop attending to that bowl of oil and out of negligence turn his attention outwards?”
sn47.20:2.11 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, venerable sir.”
sn47.20:3.1 | S: “I’ve made up this simile to make a point. | T: “I have made up this simile, bhikkhus, in order to convey a meaning.
sn47.20:3.2 | S: And this is what it means. | T: This here is the meaning:
sn47.20:3.3 | S: ‘A bowl of oil filled to the brim’ is a term for mindfulness of the body. | T: ‘The bowl of oil filled to the brim’: this is a designation for mindfulness directed to the body.
sn47.20:3.4 | S: So you should train like this: | T: Therefore, bhikkhus, you should train yourselves thus:
sn47.20:3.5 | S: ‘We will develop mindfulness of the body. We’ll cultivate it, make it our vehicle and our basis, keep it up, consolidate it, and properly implement it.’ | T: ‘We will develop and cultivate mindfulness directed to the body, make it our vehicle, make it our basis, stabilize it, exercise ourselves in it, and fully perfect it.’
sn47.20:3.6 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: Thus, bhikkhus, should you train yourselves.”
sn47.20:3.7 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
sn47.20:3.8 | P: Nālandavaggo dutiyo. | T: (none)
sn47.20:4.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn47.20:4.1 | P: Mahāpuriso nālandaṁ, | T: (none)
sn47.20:4.2 | P: Cundo celañca bāhiyo; | T: (none)

