 succeeded in 0ms:
sn47.20:4.3 | P: Uttiyo ariyo brahmā, | T: (none)
sn47.20:4.4 | P: Sedakaṁ janapadena cāti. | T: (none)

## sn47.21
sn47.21:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn47.21:1.2 | S: At one time the venerables Ānanda and Bhadda were staying near Pāṭaliputta, in the Chicken Monastery. | T: On one occasion the Venerable Ānanda and the Venerable Bhadda were dwelling at Paṭaliputta in the Cock’s Park.
sn47.21:1.3 | S: Then in the late afternoon, Venerable Bhadda came out of retreat, went to Venerable Ānanda, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then, in the evening, the Venerable Bhadda emerged from seclusion, approached the Venerable Ānanda, and exchanged greetings with him.
sn47.21:1.4 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to Ānanda: | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, he sat down to one side and said to the Venerable Ānanda:
sn47.21:1.5 | S: “Reverend Ānanda, the Buddha has spoken of skillful ethics. What’s their purpose?” | T: “Friend Ānanda, as to the wholesome virtues spoken of by the Blessed One, what is the purpose for which they were spoken of by him?”
sn47.21:2.1 | S: “Good, good, Reverend Bhadda! | T: “Good, good, friend Bhadda!
sn47.21:2.2 | S: Your approach and eloquence are excellent, and it’s a good question. | T: Your intelligence is excellent, your ingenuity is excellent, your inquiry is a good one.
sn47.21:2.3 | S: For you asked: | T: For you have asked me:
sn47.21:2.4 | S: ‘The Buddha has spoken of skillful ethics. What’s their purpose?’” | T: ‘Friend Ānanda, as to the wholesome virtues spoken of by the Blessed One, what is the purpose for which they were spoken of by him?’”
sn47.21:2.5 | S: “Yes, reverend.” | T: “Yes, friend.”
sn47.21:2.6 | S: “The Buddha has spoken of skillful ethics to the extent necessary for developing the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “Those wholesome virtues spoken of by the Blessed One were spoken of by him for the purpose of developing the four establishments of mindfulness.
sn47.21:3.1 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn47.21:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, friend, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body …
sn47.21:3.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: feelings in feelings …
sn47.21:3.4 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.21:3.5 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.21:3.6 | S: The Buddha has spoken of skillful ethics to the extent necessary for developing the four kinds of mindfulness meditation.” | T: “Those virtues spoken of by the Blessed One were spoken of by him for the sake of developing these four establishments of mindfulness.”
sn47.21:3.7 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn47.22
sn47.22:1.1 | S: The same setting. | T: The same setting.
sn47.22:1.2 | S: Venerable Bhadda said to Venerable Ānanda: | T: Sitting to one side the Venerable Bhadda said to the Venerable Ānanda:
sn47.22:1.3 | S: “What is the cause, Reverend Ānanda, what is the reason why the true teaching does not last long after the final quenching of the Realized One? | T: “Friend Ānanda, what is the cause and reason why the true Dhamma does not endure long after a Tathagata has attained final Nibbāna?
sn47.22:1.4 | S: What is the cause, what is the reason why the true teaching does last long after the final quenching of the Realized One?” | T: And what is the cause and reason why the true Dhamma endures long after a Tathagata has attained final Nibbāna?”
sn47.22:2.1 | S: “Good, good, Reverend Bhadda! | T: “Good, good, friend Bhadda!
sn47.22:2.2 | S: Your approach and eloquence are excellent, and it’s a good question. | T: Your intelligence is excellent, your acumen is excellent, your inquiry is a good one.
sn47.22:2.3 | S: For you asked: | T: For you have asked me:
sn47.22:2.4 | S: ‘What is the cause, Reverend Ānanda, what is the reason why the true teaching does not last long after the final quenching of the Realized One? | T: ‘Friend Ānanda, what is the cause and reason why the true Dhamma does not endure long after a Tathagata has attained final Nibbāna?
sn47.22:2.5 | S: What is the cause, what is the reason why the true teaching does last long after the final quenching of the Realized One?’” | T: And what is the cause and reason why the true Dhamma endures long after a Tathagata has attained final Nibbāna?’”
sn47.22:2.6 | S: “Yes, reverend.” | T: “Yes, friend.”
sn47.22:2.7 | S: “It’s because of not developing and cultivating the four kinds of mindfulness meditation that the true teaching doesn’t last long after the final quenching of the Realized One. | T: “It is, friend, because the four establishments of mindfulness are not developed and cultivated that the true Dhamma does not endure long after a Tathagata has attained final Nibbāna.
sn47.22:2.8 | S: It’s because of developing and cultivating the four kinds of mindfulness meditation that the true teaching does last long after the final quenching of the Realized One. | T: And it is because the four establishments of mindfulness are developed and cultivated that the true Dhamma endures long after a Tathagata has attained final Nibbāna.
sn47.22:3.1 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn47.22:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, friend, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body …
sn47.22:3.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: feelings in feelings …
sn47.22:3.4 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.22:3.5 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.22:3.6 | S: It’s because of not developing and cultivating these four kinds of mindfulness meditation that the true teaching doesn’t last long after the final quenching of the Realized One. | T: “It is because these four establishments of mindfulness are not developed and cultivated that the true Dhamma does not endure long after a Tathagata has attained final Nibbāna.
sn47.22:3.7 | S: It’s because of developing and cultivating these four kinds of mindfulness meditation that the true teaching does last long after the final quenching of the Realized One.” | T: And it is because these four establishments of mindfulness are developed and cultivated that the true Dhamma endures long after a Tathagata has attained final Nibbāna.”
sn47.22:3.8 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn47.24
sn47.24:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn47.24:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, there are these four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these four establishments of mindfulness.
sn47.24:1.3 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn47.24:1.4 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.24:1.5 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: He dwells contemplating feelings in feelings …
sn47.24:1.6 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.24:1.7 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.24:1.8 | S: These are the four kinds of mindfulness meditation.” | T: These are the four establishments of mindfulness.”
sn47.24:1.9 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn47.26
sn47.26:1.1 | S: At one time the venerables Sāriputta, Mahāmoggallāna, and Anuruddha were staying near Sāketa in the Thorny Wood. | T: On one occasion the Venerable Sāriputta and the Venerable Mahamoggallana and the Venerable Anuruddha were dwelling at Saketa in the Thornbush Grove.
sn47.26:1.2 | S: Then in the late afternoon, Sāriputta and Mahāmoggallāna came out of retreat, went to Anuruddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then, in the evening, the Venerable Sāriputta and the Venerable Mahamoggallana emerged from seclusion, approached the Venerable Anuruddha, and exchanged greetings with him.
sn47.26:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, they sat down to one side. Sāriputta said to Anuruddha: | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, they sat down to one side, and the Venerable Sāriputta said to the Venerable Anuruddha:
sn47.26:1.4 | S: “Reverend, they speak of the one called ‘a trainee’. | T: “Friend Anuruddha, it is said, ‘A trainee, a trainee.’
sn47.26:1.5 | S: How is a trainee defined?” | T: In what way, friend, is one a trainee?”
sn47.26:1.6 | S: “Reverends, a trainee is someone who has partly developed the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “It is, friend, because one has partly developed the four establishments of mindfulness that one is a trainee.
sn47.26:2.1 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn47.26:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, friends, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body …
sn47.26:2.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: feelings in feelings …
sn47.26:2.4 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.26:2.5 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.26:2.6 | S: A trainee is someone who has partly developed the four kinds of mindfulness meditation.” | T: It is because one has partly developed these four establishments of mindfulness that one is a trainee.”
sn47.26:2.7 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn47.29
sn47.29:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Ānanda was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Venerable Ānanda was dwelling at Rajagaha, in the Bamboo Grove, the Squirrel Sanctuary.
sn47.29:1.2 | S: Now at that time the householder Sirivaḍḍha was sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: Now on that occasion the householder Sirivaḍḍha was sick, afflicted, gravely ill.
sn47.29:1.3 | S: Then he addressed a man: | T: Then the householder Sirivaḍḍha addressed a man thus:
sn47.29:1.4 | S: “Please, my man, go to Venerable Ānanda, and in my name bow with your head to his feet. Say to him: | T: “Come, good man, approach the Venerable Ānanda, pay homage to him in my name with your head at his feet, and say:
sn47.29:1.5 | S: ‘Sir, the householder Sirivaḍḍha is sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: ‘Venerable sir, the householder Sirivaḍḍha is sick, afflicted, gravely ill;
sn47.29:1.6 | S: He bows with his head to your feet.’ | T: he pays homage to the Venerable Ānanda with his head at his feet.’
sn47.29:1.7 | S: And then say: | T: Then say:
sn47.29:1.8 | S: ‘Sir, please visit him at his home out of sympathy.’” | T: ‘It would be good, venerable sir, if the Venerable Ānanda would come to the residence of the householder Sirivaḍḍha out of compassion.’”
sn47.29:1.9 | S: “Yes, sir,” that man replied. He did as Sirivaḍḍha asked. | T: “Yes, master,” that man replied, and he approached the Venerable Ānanda, paid homage to him, sat down to one side,
sn47.29:1.10 | P: “sirivaḍḍho, bhante, gahapati ābādhiko dukkhito bāḷhagilāno, so āyasmato ānandassa pāde sirasā vandati. | T: (none)
sn47.29:1.11 | P: Evañca vadeti: | T: and delivered his message.
sn47.29:1.12 | P: ‘sādhu kira, bhante, āyasmā ānando yena sirivaḍḍhassa gahapatissa nivesanaṁ tenupasaṅkamatu anukampaṁ upādāyā’”ti. | T: (none)
sn47.29:1.13 | S: Ānanda consented with silence. | T: The Venerable Ānanda consented by silence.
sn47.29:2.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, went to the home of the householder Sirivaḍḍha, sat down on the seat spread out, and said to him: | T: Then, in the morning, the Venerable Ānanda dressed and, taking bowl and robe, went to the residence of the householder Sirivaḍḍha. He then sat down in the appointed seat and said to the householder Sirivaḍḍha:
sn47.29:2.2 | S: “I hope you’re keeping well, householder, I hope you’re all right. I hope your pain is fading, not growing; that its fading is evident, not its growing.” | T: “I hope you are bearing up, householder, I hope you are getting better. I hope your painful feelings are subsiding and not increasing, and that their subsiding, not their increase, is to be discerned.”
sn47.29:2.3 | S: “Sir, I’m not keeping well, I’m not getting by. My pain is terrible and growing, not fading; its growing is evident, not its fading.” | T: “I am not bearing up, venerable sir, I am not getting better. Strong painful feelings are increasing in me, not subsiding, and their increase, not their subsiding, is to be discerned.”
sn47.29:3.1 | S: “So you should train like this: | T: “Well then, householder, you should train thus:
sn47.29:3.2 | S: ‘I’ll meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: ‘I will dwell contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.29:3.3 | S: I’ll meditate on an aspect of feelings … | T: I will dwell contemplating feelings in feelings …
sn47.29:3.4 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.29:3.5 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world.’ | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.’
sn47.29:3.6 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: It is in such a way that you should train.”
sn47.29:4.1 | S: “These four kinds of mindfulness meditation that were taught by the Buddha are found in me, and I exhibit them. | T: “Venerable sir, as to these four establishments of mindfulness taught by the Blessed One—these things exist in me, and I live in conformity with those things.
sn47.29:4.2 | S: For I meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: I dwell, venerable sir, contemplating the body in the body …
sn47.29:4.3 | S: I meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: feelings in feelings …
sn47.29:4.4 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.29:4.5 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.29:4.6 | S: And of the five lower fetters taught by the Buddha, I don’t see any that I haven’t given up.” | T: And as to these five lower fetters taught by the Blessed One, I do not see any of these unabandoned in myself.”
sn47.29:4.7 | S: “You’re fortunate, householder, so very fortunate! | T: “It is a gain for you, householder! It is well gained by you, householder!
sn47.29:4.8 | S: You have declared the fruit of non-return.” | T: You have declared, householder, the fruit of nonreturning.”
sn47.29:4.9 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)


